# Java code written by the agent

1. **The codebase wins.** Before writing a class, open its nearest sibling (same layer, same package family) and match: package layout, naming suffixes, injection style, logging framework and message style, exception types, null handling, use of records/Lombok, formatting, test style. These defaults apply only where the codebase has no convention.
2. Match the project's language level and namespace: check `maven.compiler.release` / toolchain and `javax.*` vs `jakarta.*` before using records, text blocks, `var`, switch expressions or new APIs.
3. Constructor injection; no field `@Autowired` in new classes unless every sibling uses it.
4. Money: `BigDecimal` with explicit scale and `RoundingMode` identical to the source. Never `double` for amounts.
5. Dates: `java.time` with explicit `ZoneId` / pattern identical to the source; inject `Clock` where "now" matters for tests.
6. Externalize environment-specific values (paths, URLs, cron, zones) into properties with the same naming scheme as existing keys.
7. Log with the existing logger; no `System.out`; never log secrets or full personal records.
8. Every new rule, delta and requirement gets a focused test; reuse the project's test base classes and fixtures.
9. No new libraries without an approved decision record.
